1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,692
Why would thousands of people stand in

2
00:00:02,692 --> 00:00:04,227
the snow before dawn,

3
00:00:04,227 --> 00:00:07,324
in the capital of the Soviet Union,

4
00:00:07,324 --> 00:00:09,745
just to buy a hamburger?

5
00:00:09,983 --> 00:00:11,902
On January thirty-first,

6
00:00:11,902 --> 00:00:13,577
nineteen ninety,

7
00:00:13,577 --> 00:00:16,249
people began lining up near Pushkin Square

8
00:00:16,249 --> 00:00:18,434
in Moscow as early as four in

9
00:00:18,434 --> 00:00:19,763
the morning.

10
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:21,503
By the end of the day,

11
00:00:21,503 --> 00:00:23,720
thirty thousand customers had walked

12
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,493
through the doors of the first McDonald's

13
00:00:25,493 --> 00:00:27,023
in the Soviet Union,

14
00:00:27,023 --> 00:00:29,461
a record for any opening day in

15
00:00:29,461 --> 00:00:31,613
the company's history.

16
00:00:31,853 --> 00:00:32,979
For a few years,

17
00:00:32,979 --> 00:00:35,505
that hamburger became a symbol of the

18
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:37,713
Cold War coming to an end.

19
00:00:37,913 --> 00:00:39,457
Thirty-two years later,

20
00:00:39,457 --> 00:00:42,433
it became a symbol of something else.

21
00:00:42,883 --> 00:00:49,800
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:03,969
How do you open a burger restaurant

23
00:01:03,969 --> 00:01:06,888
in a country where private business is

24
00:01:06,888 --> 00:01:08,550
not allowed?

25
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:11,760
Very slowly.

26
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:13,997
The idea started in the nineteen

27
00:01:13,997 --> 00:01:14,784
seventies,

28
00:01:14,784 --> 00:01:17,200
with the Canadian arm of McDonald's.

29
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:19,864
Beginning in nineteen seventy-six,

30
00:01:19,864 --> 00:01:22,050
one of its executives made the first

31
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:23,818
of dozens of trips to Moscow,

32
00:01:23,818 --> 00:01:26,203
to lobby the officials of the Communist

33
00:01:26,203 --> 00:01:27,200
Party.

34
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,165
In the Soviet Union,

35
00:01:29,165 --> 00:01:30,850
the state owned the shops,

36
00:01:30,850 --> 00:01:33,160
the farms and the factories.

37
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,767
A foreign company could not just rent

38
00:01:35,767 --> 00:01:38,024
a building and start selling.

39
00:01:38,284 --> 00:01:40,907
The door opened only when the rules

40
00:01:40,907 --> 00:01:41,884
changed.

41
00:01:41,884 --> 00:01:43,716
In the late nineteen eighties,

42
00:01:43,716 --> 00:01:45,137
under perestroika,

43
00:01:45,137 --> 00:01:47,401
the Soviet Union began to allow joint

44
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:49,854
ventures with foreign companies.

45
00:01:50,054 --> 00:01:51,523
On April twenty-ninth,

46
00:01:51,523 --> 00:01:53,205
nineteen eighty-seven,

47
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:55,334
McDonald's Canada signed one with the

48
00:01:55,334 --> 00:01:57,424
Moscow city government.

49
00:01:57,524 --> 00:01:59,124
But there was a catch.

50
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:01,670
The city kept the controlling share,

51
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:03,294
fifty-one percent.

52
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:05,250
The foreign partner was limited to

53
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:06,421
forty-nine.

54
00:02:06,684 --> 00:02:09,514
Let me draw how that deal worked.

55
00:02:09,514 --> 00:02:10,040
On one side,

56
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:10,970
the city of Moscow,

57
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:11,934
with fifty-one percent.

58
00:02:11,934 --> 00:02:13,202
On the other,

59
00:02:13,202 --> 00:02:14,729
McDonald's Canada,

60
00:02:14,729 --> 00:02:16,054
with forty-nine.

61
00:02:16,054 --> 00:02:16,819
Between them,

62
00:02:16,819 --> 00:02:17,781
one company,

63
00:02:17,781 --> 00:02:19,474
which would own the restaurant.

64
00:02:19,474 --> 00:02:21,498
The city brought the land,

65
00:02:21,498 --> 00:02:25,324
the permits and the political cover.

66
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:26,146
McDonald's brought the money,

67
00:02:26,146 --> 00:02:27,424
the recipes and the know-how.

68
00:02:27,424 --> 00:02:29,934
And neither could do it alone.

69
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:31,192
In the end,

70
00:02:31,192 --> 00:02:33,233
it took fourteen years of work,

71
00:02:33,233 --> 00:02:35,817
and an investment of fifty million

72
00:02:35,817 --> 00:02:36,567
dollars,

73
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:39,234
before the first burger was sold.

74
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:41,268
And a big part of that money

75
00:02:41,268 --> 00:02:43,424
did not go into the restaurant at

76
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:44,239
all.

77
00:02:44,484 --> 00:02:47,069
Because there was a problem nobody could

78
00:02:47,069 --> 00:02:48,844
negotiate away.

79
00:02:49,204 --> 00:02:52,063
The country had no reliable supply of

80
00:02:52,063 --> 00:02:54,482
the food a burger chain needs.

81
00:03:00,117 --> 00:03:02,110
What do you do when the country

82
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:04,702
cannot supply your ingredients?

83
00:03:09,117 --> 00:03:11,663
A hamburger chain depends on the same

84
00:03:11,663 --> 00:03:12,146
bun,

85
00:03:12,146 --> 00:03:14,667
the same beef and the same potato,

86
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:15,914
every day,

87
00:03:15,914 --> 00:03:17,787
in huge quantities.

88
00:03:18,017 --> 00:03:19,914
In the Soviet planned economy,

89
00:03:19,914 --> 00:03:21,907
nobody could guarantee that.

90
00:03:22,007 --> 00:03:24,775
So McDonald's decided to build the supply

91
00:03:24,775 --> 00:03:26,129
chain itself.

92
00:03:26,384 --> 00:03:28,562
In nineteen eighty-nine,

93
00:03:28,562 --> 00:03:30,768
work began on a giant food plant

94
00:03:30,768 --> 00:03:32,120
in Solntsevo,

95
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,374
on the edge of Moscow.

96
00:03:34,494 --> 00:03:36,829
It covered about one hundred thousand

97
00:03:36,829 --> 00:03:37,854
square feet.

98
00:03:38,014 --> 00:03:39,548
According to CBC,

99
00:03:39,548 --> 00:03:41,629
it was built to turn out fourteen

100
00:03:41,629 --> 00:03:43,163
thousand buns an hour,

101
00:03:43,163 --> 00:03:45,844
and ten thousand patties an hour.

102
00:03:45,934 --> 00:03:48,727
The building opened in nineteen ninety,

103
00:03:48,727 --> 00:03:50,935
just in time for the restaurant.

104
00:03:51,184 --> 00:03:54,309
Even the potatoes had to be imported,

105
00:03:54,309 --> 00:03:55,394
as seeds.

106
00:03:55,444 --> 00:03:57,716
McDonald's brought in Russet Burbank seed

107
00:03:57,716 --> 00:03:58,701
potatoes,

108
00:03:58,701 --> 00:04:01,200
the variety used for its fries,

109
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:03,977
and trained Soviet collective farmers in

110
00:04:03,977 --> 00:04:06,344
how to grow bigger harvests.

111
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:08,837
A burger restaurant was teaching farming

112
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:10,698
to a superpower.

113
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:13,050
Let me draw the chain.

114
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,194
Here are the farms,

115
00:04:14,194 --> 00:04:16,840
with the imported potato seeds.

116
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,987
The crop goes into the plant,

117
00:04:18,987 --> 00:04:20,530
which makes the buns,

118
00:04:20,530 --> 00:04:22,700
the patties and the fries.

119
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:23,907
And from the plant,

120
00:04:23,907 --> 00:04:26,032
the food goes to the restaurant on

121
00:04:26,032 --> 00:04:27,340
Pushkin Square.

122
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:29,528
Every link in the chain was built

123
00:04:29,528 --> 00:04:30,188
from scratch,

124
00:04:30,188 --> 00:04:33,292
before a single customer walked in.

125
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:36,720
By the end of January nineteen ninety,

126
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:38,460
the chain was ready.

127
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:41,321
Now the question was whether anyone would

128
00:04:41,321 --> 00:04:42,189
come.

129
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:50,348
How many people will queue in the

130
00:04:50,348 --> 00:04:52,891
snow for a taste of the West?

131
00:04:56,850 --> 00:04:58,249
The answer was,

132
00:04:58,249 --> 00:04:59,420
thousands.

133
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:01,175
On January thirty-first,

134
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:03,578
the first people arrived around four in

135
00:05:03,578 --> 00:05:04,430
the morning,

136
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:06,340
in the dark and the cold.

137
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:08,328
By the time the doors opened,

138
00:05:08,328 --> 00:05:10,970
thousands of people were waiting in line.

139
00:05:11,230 --> 00:05:11,886
Inside,

140
00:05:11,886 --> 00:05:14,148
the restaurant had twenty-seven cash

141
00:05:14,148 --> 00:05:16,332
registers ready for them.

142
00:05:16,584 --> 00:05:19,359
The staff had expected to serve around

143
00:05:19,359 --> 00:05:21,624
ten thousand meals that day.

144
00:05:21,764 --> 00:05:24,084
They served thirty thousand.

145
00:05:24,364 --> 00:05:26,965
No McDonald's anywhere in the world had

146
00:05:26,965 --> 00:05:30,087
ever served so many customers on its

147
00:05:30,087 --> 00:05:31,207
first day.

148
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:34,405
Let me draw what opening day looked

149
00:05:34,405 --> 00:05:35,300
like.

150
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:36,719
Here is the plan,

151
00:05:36,719 --> 00:05:38,420
ten thousand meals.

152
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:40,750
And here is what actually happened.

153
00:05:40,970 --> 00:05:42,680
Thirty thousand.

154
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:44,220
Three times the plan,

155
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:46,283
from a single restaurant,

156
00:05:46,283 --> 00:05:48,188
in a single day.

157
00:05:48,450 --> 00:05:50,350
And it was not cheap.

158
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:51,883
According to CBC,

159
00:05:51,883 --> 00:05:53,961
a meal cost about half a day's

160
00:05:53,961 --> 00:05:55,860
average wages.

161
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:58,419
One man outside told a reporter that

162
00:05:58,419 --> 00:06:00,410
they were being ripped off.

163
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:01,187
Still,

164
00:06:01,187 --> 00:06:03,330
the crowds kept coming.

165
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,654
For many people,

166
00:06:04,654 --> 00:06:07,106
it was not really about the food.

167
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:09,668
The restaurant's first manager put it

168
00:06:09,668 --> 00:06:10,500
best.

169
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,455
Many people talk about perestroika,

170
00:06:13,455 --> 00:06:14,126
he said,

171
00:06:14,126 --> 00:06:16,810
but for them it is an abstraction.

172
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:19,530
I can touch my perestroika.

173
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:21,120
I can taste it.

174
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:24,208
Big Mac is perestroika.

175
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:27,191
Even the country's leadership saw it that

176
00:06:27,191 --> 00:06:28,040
way.

177
00:06:28,150 --> 00:06:30,276
Mikhail Gorbachev later called the

178
00:06:30,276 --> 00:06:31,743
restaurant a breakthrough,

179
00:06:31,743 --> 00:06:33,868
one which showed that new economic

180
00:06:33,868 --> 00:06:36,419
relations between the Soviet Union and the

181
00:06:36,419 --> 00:06:38,720
rest of the world were possible.

182
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:40,871
A hamburger had become a piece of

183
00:06:40,871 --> 00:06:42,532
foreign policy.

184
00:06:42,784 --> 00:06:44,997
Less than two years later,

185
00:06:44,997 --> 00:06:48,484
the Soviet Union itself was gone.

186
00:06:48,594 --> 00:06:50,828
The restaurant stayed.

187
00:06:56,484 --> 00:06:58,967
What happens to a symbol when the

188
00:06:58,967 --> 00:07:01,498
country around it changes?

189
00:07:05,484 --> 00:07:07,355
In the new Russia,

190
00:07:07,355 --> 00:07:10,174
McDonald's slowly took control.

191
00:07:10,334 --> 00:07:13,156
In February nineteen ninety-six,

192
00:07:13,156 --> 00:07:15,290
it bought most of the city's shares

193
00:07:15,290 --> 00:07:16,690
in the joint venture,

194
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:18,824
raising its own stake from forty-nine

195
00:07:18,824 --> 00:07:21,084
percent to eighty percent.

196
00:07:21,214 --> 00:07:23,185
The company that had once needed

197
00:07:23,185 --> 00:07:26,031
permission to open a single restaurant now

198
00:07:26,031 --> 00:07:27,726
ran the business.

199
00:07:27,984 --> 00:07:29,204
And it grew.

200
00:07:29,204 --> 00:07:30,725
By twenty twenty-two,

201
00:07:30,725 --> 00:07:32,710
McDonald's had more than eight hundred and

202
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:35,074
fifty restaurants in Russia.

203
00:07:35,104 --> 00:07:37,392
It owned about eighty-four percent of

204
00:07:37,392 --> 00:07:37,787
them,

205
00:07:37,787 --> 00:07:39,430
and ran most of them itself,

206
00:07:39,430 --> 00:07:41,694
rather than through franchise owners.

207
00:07:41,724 --> 00:07:44,654
They employed sixty-two thousand people.

208
00:07:44,744 --> 00:07:47,226
Together with its restaurants in Ukraine,

209
00:07:47,226 --> 00:07:49,520
the business brought in about nine percent

210
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:51,584
of the company's annual revenue,

211
00:07:51,584 --> 00:07:53,644
around two billion dollars.

212
00:07:53,864 --> 00:07:55,876
For a brand built on burgers and

213
00:07:55,876 --> 00:07:56,353
fries,

214
00:07:56,353 --> 00:07:58,616
Russia had become one of its big

215
00:07:58,616 --> 00:07:59,719
markets.

216
00:07:59,984 --> 00:08:03,084
Let me draw how the business changed.

217
00:08:03,104 --> 00:08:04,791
In nineteen ninety,

218
00:08:04,791 --> 00:08:05,838
one restaurant,

219
00:08:05,838 --> 00:08:07,525
forty-nine percent owned,

220
00:08:07,525 --> 00:08:09,834
in a joint venture with the state.

221
00:08:09,944 --> 00:08:11,595
By twenty twenty-two,

222
00:08:11,595 --> 00:08:13,509
more than eight hundred and fifty

223
00:08:13,509 --> 00:08:14,442
restaurants,

224
00:08:14,442 --> 00:08:17,674
mostly owned and run by McDonald's itself.

225
00:08:17,914 --> 00:08:19,867
That is what made the next step

226
00:08:19,867 --> 00:08:21,582
so expensive.

227
00:08:21,817 --> 00:08:22,324
Then,

228
00:08:22,324 --> 00:08:24,967
in February twenty twenty-two,

229
00:08:24,967 --> 00:08:27,436
Russia invaded Ukraine.

230
00:08:33,084 --> 00:08:35,531
What does it cost to walk away

231
00:08:35,531 --> 00:08:38,578
from thirty years of business?

232
00:08:42,084 --> 00:08:42,938
On March eighth,

233
00:08:42,938 --> 00:08:44,229
twenty twenty-two,

234
00:08:44,229 --> 00:08:45,980
McDonald's announced that it was

235
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:48,606
temporarily closing its restaurants in

236
00:08:48,606 --> 00:08:49,644
Russia.

237
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:51,185
Two months later,

238
00:08:51,185 --> 00:08:52,407
on May sixteenth,

239
00:08:52,407 --> 00:08:54,004
it went further.

240
00:08:54,064 --> 00:08:56,120
After more than thirty years in the

241
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:56,783
country,

242
00:08:56,783 --> 00:08:57,445
it said,

243
00:08:57,445 --> 00:08:59,250
it would leave Russia for good,

244
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:01,306
and it expected to book a charge

245
00:09:01,306 --> 00:09:03,133
of about one point two to one

246
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:05,104
point four billion dollars.

247
00:09:05,304 --> 00:09:08,018
Its chief executive wrote that the company

248
00:09:08,018 --> 00:09:10,279
could no longer keep the Arches shining

249
00:09:10,279 --> 00:09:11,106
there.

250
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:12,502
Three days later,

251
00:09:12,502 --> 00:09:14,618
McDonald's agreed to sell the whole

252
00:09:14,618 --> 00:09:17,181
business to Alexander Govor,

253
00:09:17,181 --> 00:09:19,533
a local businessman who had run

254
00:09:19,533 --> 00:09:22,354
twenty-five restaurants in Siberia as a

255
00:09:22,354 --> 00:09:24,130
McDonald's licensee.

256
00:09:24,670 --> 00:09:27,019
The deal kept the employees in their

257
00:09:27,019 --> 00:09:29,380
jobs for at least two years.

258
00:09:29,430 --> 00:09:32,189
The price was never made public.

259
00:09:32,450 --> 00:09:35,450
Let me draw what leaving cost.

260
00:09:35,660 --> 00:09:36,838
Here is McDonald's,

261
00:09:36,838 --> 00:09:39,217
and here is the Russian business,

262
00:09:39,217 --> 00:09:41,139
more than eight hundred and fifty

263
00:09:41,139 --> 00:09:42,460
restaurants.

264
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:44,634
An arrow to the new owner,

265
00:09:44,634 --> 00:09:47,250
for a price nobody disclosed.

266
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:48,574
And an arrow back,

267
00:09:48,574 --> 00:09:50,410
to the company's books.

268
00:09:50,410 --> 00:09:52,342
A charge of one point three billion

269
00:09:52,342 --> 00:09:53,043
dollars,

270
00:09:53,043 --> 00:09:54,976
mostly to write off what it had

271
00:09:54,976 --> 00:09:57,025
invested in Russia.

272
00:09:57,284 --> 00:09:58,424
In its results,

273
00:09:58,424 --> 00:10:00,518
McDonald's booked a charge of one point

274
00:10:00,518 --> 00:10:03,077
two billion dollars in the second quarter

275
00:10:03,077 --> 00:10:04,683
of twenty twenty-two,

276
00:10:04,683 --> 00:10:07,009
and one point three billion dollars over

277
00:10:07,009 --> 00:10:08,615
the first half of the year,

278
00:10:08,615 --> 00:10:11,407
related to the sale of its business

279
00:10:11,407 --> 00:10:12,705
in Russia.

280
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:16,240
And then came the strangest part.

281
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:17,243
On June twelfth,

282
00:10:17,243 --> 00:10:18,729
twenty twenty-two,

283
00:10:18,729 --> 00:10:21,500
the restaurants began to reopen under

284
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:22,735
their new owner,

285
00:10:22,735 --> 00:10:25,254
starting with the very same building on

286
00:10:25,254 --> 00:10:26,590
Pushkin Square.

287
00:10:26,590 --> 00:10:27,781
Same address,

288
00:10:27,781 --> 00:10:29,584
and under the deal,

289
00:10:29,584 --> 00:10:31,100
the same staff.

290
00:10:31,330 --> 00:10:33,710
Only the name had changed.

291
00:10:33,830 --> 00:10:35,630
It was announced just a couple of

292
00:10:35,630 --> 00:10:37,670
hours before the doors opened.

293
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:40,220
Vkusno and tochka.

294
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,132
Roughly,

295
00:10:41,132 --> 00:10:42,045
tasty,

296
00:10:42,045 --> 00:10:43,588
and that's it.

297
00:10:43,850 --> 00:10:45,970
So what does one hamburger restaurant

298
00:10:45,970 --> 00:10:48,690
teach us about the world economy?

299
00:10:48,950 --> 00:10:51,150
That a business is never only a

300
00:10:51,150 --> 00:10:52,350
